Sources for `void-autopilot` (see `docs/plans/skill-audits/void-autopilot.md` for the audit):

- void-harness `backlog-autopilot` (this repo, being replaced) — the attended parallel burst,
  its worktree isolation and its human confirmation flow. Autopilot keeps the shape and drops
  the parts that never earned their keep: headless mode, auto-merge, the streaming surface.
- Anthropic, "Claude Code best practices" + the Workflow tool — deterministic multi-agent
  orchestration with per-agent worktree isolation, the substrate that recovers determinism a
  long in-session LLM orchestrator loses.
- git worktrees (native) — per-ticket isolation so parallel edits cannot corrupt one working
  tree. The isolation is real filesystem isolation, not a runtime promise.
- Codex subagents (native, enabled by default in local releases) — the second runtime's
  equivalent primitive. Subagents share filesystem access, so the isolation comes from the
  pre-created worktrees and explicit workdirs, never from an assumed sandbox.
- Two-phase commit / lease literature, adapted down — the tracker offers no multi-issue
  transaction, so the protocol is logically atomic (observe, act idempotently, re-observe,
  compensate) rather than transactionally atomic. What was rejected: any wording that claims
  transport-level atomicity.
